well let's speak to the former treasury  minister and former housing secretary  robert jenner mr generic always great to  see you thanks for joining us on this  budget day who's going to be better off  today  well i think there's an uncertain  picture across the world with inflation  with shortages and issues that the  chancellor have to contend with but as  your report just said there are reasons  for optimism unemployment in particular  is much lower than people expected and  the british economy is growing it's  going to be the fastest growing of any  g7 economy so i think that does give the  chancellor some room room for two things  really firstly for some more investment  in public services to tackle the cost of  living and a proper plan to grow the  economy as we move forwards but he's  going to have to balance that with the  need to be fiscally responsible because  of that continued uncertainty and i  think that's the backdrop which the  chancellor has been considering in the  weeks that have preceded the budget  so who's going to be better off today  well i think we're we're all going to be  better off in the sense that it looks  like the opr forecasts are going to show  that the economy has grown substantially  therefore we're less we're borrowing  much less money as a country and that  gives some more room for the chancellor  to invest in the future of public  services which we all depend on like the  nhs but i think he's also going to have  to think carefully about those people on  the most modest incomes and how he can  help them to whether what's undoubtedly  still going to be a difficult period  when we're seeing inflation rising  higher energy prices and so on  you've seen that in some of the  announcements that have already been  made like the decision to increase the  national living wage which is going to  lead to an increase in wages for about  two million people across the country  and the decision to move to a more  normal period for public sector workers  as well so that there won't be a a  freeze there but the pay review bodies  will be deciding in the months to come  what's a sensible and affordable  increase in wages for that six million  people right across the country so i  think it's striking that balance  along with as i say  continuing to be fiscally prudent  because we just don't know what the  future holds and inflation means the  risk of interest rate rises okay and  that's very costly given that we're all  into the chancellor's books sure given  that we're all going to be better off um  public sector workers are going to get  an above inflation pay rise  well we don't know and we can't say that  yet can we to be fair to the chancellor  everybody's going to get it it's got to  be higher than inflation hasn't it  well the way that public sector pay is  set in this country is by pay review  bodies who then advise the relevant  government department through a proper  process and those are independent bodies  that include trade unionists business  people experts as i think what the  chancellor is going to say is that we're  able as a result of the growing economy  to move into a more normal period that  means that he's going to be asking those  pay review bodies to come forward with  their proposals and then he'll consider  those all the relevant government  departments will consider them next year  so i don't think he'll be in the  position to say exactly uh what you  would like him to say just yet but  that's going to be a few months off  so people just have to wait a bit longer  but yeah i think as a result of it  has been managed  last year just let me say and you can of  course override those pay review bodies  as we all know if that's what you choose  to do either more in favor of the  workers or less as indeed we saw last  year unless it is above inflation pay  rise you said everybody's going to be  better off unless it's an above impact  inflation pay rise then we're not all  going to be better off are we  well i said that i think there'll be  good news across the board for the  economy and for members of public  because we'll be able to invest more in  public services and be more fiscally  prudent so that we've got some  protection built into the economy should  there be shocks down the line i think it  is good news given the cost of living  challenge that we have right now that we  are going to be able to increase wages  uh either through the national living  wage going up that's a very significant  development probably the most  significant one because that's going to  lead to the people who are on the lowest  wages many of whom are within the public  sector like care workers for example but  others across the board those people are  going to be seeing a pay rise of around  six and a half percent that's very  significant increase and really welcome  given what's happening uh for the cost  of living we were all good the public  says that's what you said  i think i think we're just going i think  we're all just going to have to wait and  see what the pay review bodies report  back and there'd be no point  circumventing that process given that  it's been set up in that way  why is the gender pay gap still at 7.9  according to the ons office for national  statistics i mean that has been illegal  since 1970 it's increased during the  pandemic why is this government still  condoning men being paid more than women  well the government doesn't condone men  being paid it's against the law what are  you doing about it  it's been against the law for a very  long time and we've been bearing down  upon it there are structural issues  within the economy about the different  types of jobs that sometimes men and  women do but we as a government have  been trying to tackle this for a long  time it is coming down uh it has been  coming down over the course of the last  10 years but it's still unacceptable  over the last year you and i have gone  up it's gone up over the last year it's  increased to 7.9 percent  well that may be the case over the last  year but the longer term trend is is  downwards uh but i'm not i'm not here to  defend that absolutely not it's  completely wrong we want to see more  women in the workplace and those who've  been properly rewarded for the work that  they do and it's for employers uh to  make sure that they're doing that yeah  but it's also against the law and it has  been for 50 years so you would have  hoped that perhaps it would have gone by  now but let's wait and see  test and trace has been described by the  pm as world beating a parliamentary  investigation says it's an eye-watering  waste of taxpayers money which is it  well i've seen the reporting on the the  public accounts committee's work this  morning and that's something i'm  certainly going to to read it is very  concerning what they've said on the  other hand it is a program that i think  has led to 320 million tests being done  over the course of the last year and a  year and a bit uh 20 million people have  been contacted so it has played an  important part in tackling the pandemic  are there lessons that can and must be  learned from it i think absolutely and  we're all going to have to pay  particularly the government to what's  said by the public accounts committee  okay slightly running out of time so i  can't press you further on that but what  i can ask you about is our most  important diplomat her majesty she is  not going to be at cop 26. the pm said  to a group of kids in downing street  it's touch and go if cop 26 will be a  success and he is very worried about it  given that her majesty is now also not  going how  much do you think it's going to be a  success or a failure what's the  likelihood  well firstly like you i said my best  wishes to her majesty the queen i know  she would have liked to have been at cop  and she's going to be doing a video  message it's much more important that  she puts her own health and well-being  first  but as regards cop well i think there is  a lot more work to be done i've seen  alex sharma has been meeting chinese  representatives in the last 24 hours we  need to ensure that those countries who  are the biggest global emitters  come to the table and there will be a  lot of last minute wrangling and  negotiation to be done i don't think the  prime minister or indeed alex sharma  could have done more than they have in  terms of trying to corral global  nations to take action and there has  been some significant progress in the  last couple of days i think for example  scott morrison in australia making a  significant step to some extent saudi  arabia although it's it's to a slower  trajectory than we would have wanted but  you can see progress but a lot more now  needs to be achieved just in a couple of  days  okay  finally before i let you go why are  masks mandatory for all staff in the  house of commons contractors press and  third parties but not for mps  i i don't know is the honest answer to  that okay i mean that's a decision i  think by the house  authorities or the speaker who issued  those guidelines uh it doesn't seem  right to me that there's one rule for  everyone else who works in the building  but not for members of parliament i  think we will have to change our  behavior accordingly i wore a mask when  i was voting last night uh through the  pack division lobbies i think we'll all  have to consider wearing a mask in the  coming days  and will you wear one for the budget  i i think i will yeah because it will be  a busy occasion lots of people tightly  packed  i i don't think that as members of  parliament we can ask something  different of our staff and the people  who work in the building than we're  doing ourselves  mr generic i'm so sorry i can't talk to  you in person it's always much more fun  doing that forgive me uh for  interrupting you on occasion it's always  a little bit tricky isn't it when it's  down the line it's a pleasure talking to  you we'll see you very soon hopefully  thank you  nice to see you bye now

